        # 8
        {"kind": "h1", "text": "8. Design & UX"},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "There is no graphical interface. The consumer is an AI agent over stdio, and the operator sees only stderr. The design surface is therefore the tool contract itself, and it has three parts."},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "Tool naming. The current names are bare verbs and nouns — list_sites, inspect_url, get_position_changes — with no namespace. Every other server in the monorepo prefixes its tools (brevo_, cp_, ga_, wp_). When several servers are mounted in one session, an unprefixed list_sites or get_indexing_status is a live collision risk and gives the agent no signal about which system it is addressing. Renaming to gsc_* is the single largest UX fix available."},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "Descriptions as the only affordance. The agent cannot read the source, so a tool description is the entire user manual. Descriptions must state the date-range semantics, the 25,000-row ceiling, the 2-3 day lag, and for submit_url_for_indexing both the Owner-permission requirement and the daily quota. Anything omitted from a description is, in practice, invisible."},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "Error legibility and guard ergonomics. Because arguments are not validated, a malformed date or an unknown dimension reaches Google and returns as a raw API error the agent must interpret. Errors should be rewritten to name the offending parameter. Guards are equally thin: delete_sitemap removes a registered feed and submit_url_for_indexing spends finite quota, and neither asks for confirmation. A confirm flag on both, plus a dry-run mode on the derived analytics tools, would make the write surface safe to expose to an autonomous agent."},

